// The client and the five libraries it links against are one repository
// and six modules. This workspace is what makes a clone build without
// them being published first, and it is also what CI uses, because a
// library built by the workflow in this repository is the one that has
// to be tested rather than whatever the proxy already has.
//
// A workspace in a dependency is ignored, so this changes nothing for
// anybody who has installed the client. It goes away when the libraries
// are tagged and the go.mod requirements can stand on their own.
//
// One thing it does not cover: go mod tidy reads a module on its own
// and never the workspace around it, so until the libraries are tagged
// it will try to fetch lib/<goos>-<goarch>@v0.0.0 from the proxy and
// fail. Nothing else does, including go build, go vet, go test and the
// race detector. scripts/release.sh is what ends it, by tagging the
// libraries and rewriting the requirements to name what it tagged.

go 1.26.6

use (
	.
	./lib/darwin-amd64
	./lib/darwin-arm64
	./lib/linux-amd64
	./lib/linux-arm64
	./lib/windows-amd64
)
